Q: cs50 pset2 Vigenere cipher A: <p>After testing out your code, what is at issue here is the key offset processing is only moving the character offset by the modulo value (j % keylen) instead of the offset value of the character at the key position.</p>
<pre><code>printf(&quot;%c&quot;,((p[i] - 97) + (j % keylen) % 26) + 97);

printf(&quot;%c&quot;, ((p[i] - 65) + (j % keylen) % 26) + 65);
</code></pre>
<p>So when testing this code using a simple two-character key, following is the resulting value for an input of &quot;ABCDE&quot;.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_3/bin/Release$ ./vig_3 bc
Enter Plain Text: ABCDE
ACCEE
</code></pre>
<p>So, the first character will not be offset as &quot;j % keylen&quot; will be &quot;0&quot;, the second character will be offset by a value of &quot;1&quot;, the third character will be offset by a value of &quot;0&quot; and the modulo operation will again calculate to &quot;0, the fourth character will be offset by a value of &quot;1&quot;, and the final character again will be offset by a value of &quot;0&quot;.</p>
<p>What should be happening is the character value of the key at position &quot;j&quot; should be used to derive an offset value to be applied to the plain text character to derive an encrypted character.  With that in mind, following is a refactored version of the program to perform the correct offset encryption.  The full source code was included to provide context, but the statements in question are the two &quot;printf&quot; statements.</p>
<pre><code>int main(int argc, string argv[])
{
    string kword = argv[1];

    if(argc !=2)
    {
        printf(&quot;Usage: ./vigenere k \n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    else
    {
        for(int i = 0, n = strlen(kword); i&lt;n; i++)
        {
            if(!isalpha(kword[i]))
            {
                printf(&quot;Usage: ./vigenere k \n&quot;);
                return 1;
            }
        }
    }

    int keylen = strlen(kword);

    string p = get_string(&quot;Enter Plain Text: &quot;);

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        for (int i = 0, j = 0, n = strlen(p); i &lt; n; i++)

        {
            if(isalpha(p[i]))
            {
                if (islower(p[i]))
                {
                    printf(&quot;%c&quot;, ((p[i] - 'a' + tolower(kword[(j % keylen)]) - 'a') % 26) + 'a');   /* Refactored   */
                    j++;
                }
                else if (isupper(p[i]))
                {
                    printf(&quot;%c&quot;, ((p[i] - 'A' + tolower(kword[(j % keylen)]) - 'a') % 26) + 'A');   /* Refactored   */
                    j++;
                }

            }
            else
                printf(&quot;%c&quot;, p[i]);
        }

        printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>As a point of clarification, this snippet of code provides the character value offset of the current key character being utilized.</p>
<pre><code>tolower(kword[(j % keylen)]) - 'a'
</code></pre>
<p>Also, to provide a better understanding of the ASCII code manipulation, constant character values like 'a' and 'A&quot; were used in lieu of a hard-coded number.</p>
<p>With that, following is a retest of the simple string encryption along with a sample encryption test from the Vigenere cipher web page which agrees with the expected outcome.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_3/bin/Release$ ./vig_3 bc
Enter Plain Text: ABCDE
BDDFF
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_3/bin/Release$ ./vig_3 oculorhinolaryngology
Enter Plain Text: attacking tonight
ovnlqbpvt hznzeuz
</code></pre>
<p>The takeaway from this would be to delve more into &quot;C&quot; tutorials in regard to ASCII character values and manipulation of those values along with tutorials on code debugging, and probably a better understanding of the Vigenere cipher process.</p>
